 "derivation":"This box condenses two footnotes of Gershevitch's Avestan Hymn to Mithra, and condenses them too far. Note 27 (p.181) is about Yt.10.34 rəxšyant-: 'I regard rəxšya- as a denominative of *rəxša- “defiance”, which in its turn would be a verbal noun formed from the -s- extension of a base *rax- or *rək-', and Gershevitch's parallels for that kind of s-extension are 'Av. daxsa- “fire, Brand” + to dag- “burn”, Sogd. ʔwyšʔ “cauldron” to pak- “cook”, and Av. fra-daxs-'. So 'rax, rak' are Gershevitch's reconstructed bases behind an attested participle, not Avestan lemmata, and their sense is 'to defy', not 'to hurt (burn)'; only daxša- 'fire' is really 'burn'. Note 394 (pp.192 f.) gives 'fradaxsanya-, adj. to fradaxsana- “sling”'; the noun itself is well attested – Bartholomae AiW s.v. 'j. fradaxšanā- f. „Schleuder“ als Waffe' (Vd.14.9, thirty sling-stones) and fradaxšanya- 'Schleuderstein' (Vd.17.9, Yt.10.39) – but Bartholomae writes flatly 'Ableit. – fra-daxš-; Et.?': he knows the derivational base and declares the etymology unknown. Nourai's gloss 'a device to hurt or strike from a distance' is a good description of the object; the claim that the base means 'burn' rests only on the analogy Gershevitch draws with daxša-, which he does not extend to fra-daxš-.",
